//? Title: Bayesian Optimization: Minimising Expensive Functions
//? Subtitle: A Gaussian-process surrogate, marginal-likelihood hyperparameters and expected improvement, derived, checked by hand, tested on functions drawn from the prior and on Branin, and used to tune kernel ridge regression on the diabetes data, using datamunge's optim::BayesianOptimization
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
How can a function be minimised when each evaluation is so expensive that only a few dozen are
affordable? Bayesian optimization models the function as a Gaussian process. It fits the
process's length scale by maximising the marginal likelihood after every evaluation, and it
evaluates next where the expected improvement over the best value so far is largest. This report
derives the posterior, the profile likelihood and the expected improvement, and works a
two-observation example by hand. The library's posterior agrees with scikit-learn to ten digits. On
functions drawn from the prior, the posterior's 95% bands cover 95% of the truth when the
hyperparameters are known. With hyperparameters fitted from five points they cover only 75%, and
94% with forty. On the Branin function, with 40 evaluations, expected improvement with a fitted
length scale gets within 0.01 of the minimum in 92% of 25 runs. Random search and a particle swarm
never do, and expected improvement with a badly fixed length scale rarely does. Tuning the two
hyperparameters of a kernel ridge regression on the diabetes data, 30 evaluations come within 0.05
of the leave-one-out error of a 900-point grid in every run.
)

\toc

> The problem

We want the minimum of a function \(f\) on a box \([a,b]\subset\mathbb{R}^d\) when each evaluation is
expensive: a simulation that runs for an hour, a laboratory experiment, or the cross-validated
error of a model that takes minutes to train. Only a few dozen evaluations are affordable. No
gradient is available, and \(f\) may have several local minima. Methods that need thousands of
evaluations, such as particle swarms, evolution strategies or a fine grid, are out of reach, and
a coarse grid wastes most of its evaluations in regions that are clearly poor.

Bayesian optimization treats the unknown \(f\) as a random function. After each evaluation it
updates a probabilistic model of \(f\) (the *surrogate*), and it chooses the next point by trading
off a low predicted value against high uncertainty. The surrogate costs little compared with an
evaluation of \(f\), so all the effort goes into choosing where to evaluate.

> The method

>> A Gaussian-process prior

The surrogate is a Gaussian process (GP): any finite set of values \(f(x_1),\dots,f(x_n)\) is jointly
normal with mean \(m\) and covariance given by the squared-exponential kernel

$$
k(x,x')=s\,\exp\!\Big(-\frac{\lVert x-x'\rVert^2}{2\ell^2}\Big).
$$
\alttext(k of x and x prime equals s times the exponential of minus the squared distance between x and x prime over 2 ell squared.)

The signal variance \(s\) sets how far \(f\) strays from \(m\), and the length scale \(\ell\) sets how far
apart two points must be before their values are nearly unrelated. Draws from this prior are
smooth, infinitely differentiable functions that wiggle on the scale \(\ell\). Observations
\(y_i=f(x_i)+\varepsilon_i\) carry noise of variance \(\sigma_n^2\); for a deterministic \(f\) this is a tiny
*nugget* that keeps the matrices well conditioned.

>> The posterior

Write \(K\) for the \(n\times n\) matrix \(k(x_i,x_j)\), \(k_*\) for the vector \(k(x_*,x_i)\) and \(A=K+\sigma_n^2I\).
Conditioning the joint normal distribution of \((y,f(x_*))\) on \(y\) gives

$$
\mu(x_*)=m+k_*^\top A^{-1}(y-m\mathbf 1),\qquad \sigma^2(x_*)=s-k_*^\top A^{-1}k_*.
$$
\alttext(The posterior mean at x star is m plus k star transpose times A inverse times y minus m. The posterior variance is s minus k star transpose times A inverse times k star.)

`RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate::fit()` factors \(A=LL^\top\) once and solves for \(\alpha=A^{-1}(y-m\mathbf 1)\).
`predict()` then costs one forward substitution per point: the mean is \(m+k_*^\top\alpha\), and the variance
is \(s-\lVert z\rVert^2\) with \(Lz=k_*\). The posterior interpolates exact observations (\(\sigma\to0\) at an observed
point as \(\sigma_n\to0\)) and returns to the prior \(N(m,s)\) far from all of them.

With `normalize_values` the values are first centred at their mean and divided by their
standard deviation. The prior mean \(m\) is then the sample mean, and \(s\) is measured in units of the
sample variance, so the same hyperparameter ranges suit any objective.

As a check, take six points with \(\ell=0.6\), \(s=1.3\) and \(\sigma_n^2=0.01\), with normalisation. The posterior
means, standard deviations and log marginal likelihood agree to ten digits with scikit-learn's
`GaussianProcessRegressor` (kernel `ConstantKernel * RBF + WhiteKernel`, `normalize_y=True`).
With the length scale fitted, both give \(\ell=0.513\) and the same maximised likelihood.

>> Hyperparameters by marginal likelihood

The prior's \(\ell\) matters more than anything else: too short and the model forgets each
observation within a short distance, too long and it smooths over the minima. Rather than guess it,
`fit_hyperparameters` maximises the log marginal likelihood of the observations,

$$
\log p(y\mid\ell,s)=-\tfrac12(y-m)^\top A^{-1}(y-m)-\tfrac12\log\lvert A\rvert-\tfrac n2\log2\pi .
$$
\alttext(The log marginal likelihood equals minus one half y minus m transposed A inverse y minus m, minus one half the log determinant of A, minus n over 2 log 2 pi.)

The first term rewards fit and the second penalises complexity: a short length scale makes \(A\)
close to diagonal, with a large determinant. With the nugget held as a fixed ratio \(g=\sigma_n^2/s\),
write \(A=s(R+gI)\). Setting the derivative in \(s\) to zero gives \(\hat s=(y-m)^\top(R+gI)^{-1}(y-m)/n\), and the
profile likelihood

$$
\log p(y\mid\ell,\hat s)=-\tfrac n2\log(2\pi\hat s)-\tfrac n2-\tfrac12\log\lvert R+gI\rvert
$$
\alttext(The profile log likelihood equals minus n over 2 log of 2 pi s hat, minus n over 2, minus one half the log determinant of R plus g I.)

depends on \(\ell\) alone. It is maximised over a 40-point log grid from 0.01 to 10 times the
widest coordinate range of the data, then refined by golden-section search. With `fit_noise`, the
ratio \(g\) is searched as well, from \(10^{-8}\) to 1. `log_marginal_likelihood()` reports the result for the
raw values, including the \(-n\log(\text{scale})\) Jacobian of the normalisation.

>> Acquisition functions

Let \(f^\star\) be the smallest value observed so far (the *incumbent*) and \(Y\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2)\) the
posterior at a candidate \(x\). The improvement is \(\max(f^\star-Y,0)\). Writing \(Y=\mu+\sigma Z\) and \(u=(f^\star-\mu)/\sigma\),

$$
\operatorname{EI}(x)=\sigma\int_{-\infty}^{u}(u-z)\varphi(z)\,dz=(f^\star-\mu)\Phi(u)+\sigma\varphi(u),
$$
\alttext(The expected improvement equals sigma times the integral from minus infinity to u of u minus z times phi of z, which equals f star minus mu times capital Phi of u plus sigma times phi of u.)

using \(\int_{-\infty}^u z\varphi(z)\,dz=-\varphi(u)\). Expected improvement (EI) grows when the predicted mean is
low (the first term, exploitation) and when the uncertainty is high (the second term,
exploration); it is zero only where \(\sigma=0\) and \(\mu\ge f^\star\). Two simpler criteria are available
through `GaussianProcessSurrogateOptions::acquisition`:

- *probability of improvement*, \(\Phi(u)\), which ignores how large the improvement would be and
  so tends to take many tiny steps near the incumbent;
- *lower confidence bound*, \(-(\mu-\kappa\sigma)\), the most optimistic plausible value; \(\kappa=0\) is greedy
  minimisation of the posterior mean, with no exploration at all.

`exploration` (\(\xi\)) shifts the target of EI and PI to \(f^\star-\xi\).

>> The loop

`BayesianOptimization::run()` evaluates `initial_samples` uniform random points. Then, for
`max_iterations` rounds, it refits the surrogate to all observations, scores `candidates` uniform
random points by the acquisition, polishes the best one by compass search on the acquisition
surface (steps of 10% of each side, halved `refine_iterations` times), and evaluates \(f\) there.
The result keeps every point, value, running minimum and acquisition value. Each fit costs
\(O(n^3)\) for the factorisation, repeated 80 times or so when fitting \(\ell\); with \(n\le100\) this is a few
milliseconds, negligible next to an expensive \(f\).

> Simple examples

>> Two observations by hand

Fix \(\ell=1\), \(s=1\), \(m=0\) and no noise (the two-argument constructor), and observe \(f(0)=1\), \(f(2)=-1\).
Then \(K=\begin{pmatrix}1&e^{-2}\\e^{-2}&1\end{pmatrix}\). At \(x_*=1\), \(k_*=e^{-1/2}(1,1)\). By antisymmetry the mean is 0, and

$$
\sigma^2(1)=1-\frac{2e^{-1}}{1+e^{-2}}=0.35195,\qquad \sigma(1)=0.5933 .
$$
\alttext(The posterior variance at 1 equals 1 minus 2 e to the minus 1 over 1 plus e to the minus 2, which is 0.35195, so the standard deviation is 0.5933.)

With incumbent \(f^\star=-1\), \(u=-1/0.5933=-1.6855\), and \(\operatorname{EI}(1)=-\Phi(-1.6855)+0.5933\,\varphi(1.6855)=-0.04594+0.05717=0.01123\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  dm::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate gp(1.0, 0.0);
  gp.fit({{0.0}, {2.0}}, {1.0, -1.0});
  std::vector<double> xs{-1.0, 0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0, 5.0}, mean, sd, ei, pi, lcb;
  for (const double x : xs) {
    const auto p = gp.predict({x});
    mean.push_back(p.mean), sd.push_back(p.sd);
    ei.push_back(dm::optim::expected_improvement(p.mean, p.sd, -1.0));
    pi.push_back(dm::optim::probability_of_improvement(p.mean, p.sd, -1.0));
    lcb.push_back(p.mean - 2.0 * p.sd);
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("x", xs);
  table.add_column("mean", mean);
  table.add_column("sd", sd);
  table.add_column("EI", ei);
  table.add_column("PI", pi);
  table.add_column("mean - 2 sd", lcb);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 5;
  md.column_digits = {{"x", 1}};
  std::print("{}\n\nLog marginal likelihood: {:.5f}\n", table.to_md(md), gp.log_marginal_likelihood());
}
```
