\image(figures/bo_1d.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: the true function (black), the GP posterior mean (blue) with a band of two posterior standard deviations, and the evaluations (red), after 4 (top) and 6 (bottom) evaluations. Right: expected improvement over the incumbent for the same surrogate. The grey line marks the next point evaluated.)
\alttext(Four panels in two rows. Top left: after four evaluations, a black curve with two dips, a blue posterior mean that follows it near the red observations on the left, and a wide pale blue band on the right where there are no observations. Top right: expected improvement with two broad humps, near x equals 0.25 and at the right edge, where a grey line marks the next evaluation at x equals 2. Bottom row: after six evaluations the band is narrow except near x equals 1.4, the second dip, where a single sharp expected-improvement peak marks the next evaluation.)

The ninth evaluation reaches \(f(-0.3597)=-0.5004\), the global minimum \(f(-0.3594)=-0.500360\) to four
digits. The table shows what the loop was doing before that. With only three points, the marginal
likelihood picks a length scale of 0.019, near the lower end of its range: three numbers carry
almost no information about smoothness, and here the likelihood happens to prefer the shortest
length scales. That makes the surrogate forget each observation within a short distance, and
the fourth point is placed next to the incumbent. From four points on, the fitted length scale
is 0.24 to 0.39. It rises to 0.95–0.98 once the global basin is sampled, and is 0.58 at the last fit.

The panels show the trade-off. After four evaluations (top), the band is wide on the right, and
EI has two broad humps of almost equal height. The right edge wins (0.0437), where the band is
widest. That evaluation, \(f(2)=2.32\), is poor, but it rules out the region. After six evaluations
(bottom), the band is narrow except around the second dip, and EI has one sharp peak there. The
seventh evaluation, \(f(1.378)=0.097\), finds the local minimum and shows that it is worse than the
incumbent. After that EI concentrates on the global basin, and its values fall to \(10^{-4}\): the
model expects little more to gain anywhere.

> Checking the theory

>> Calibration on functions drawn from the prior

If \(f\) really is a draw from the GP prior and the hyperparameters are known, the posterior is
exact: \((f(x)-\mu(x))/\sigma(x)\) is standard normal at every unobserved point, and the 95% band
\(\mu\pm1.96\sigma\) covers \(f\) 95% of the time. With \(\ell\) and \(s\) estimated from the same few points, the
plug-in posterior ignores their uncertainty and should cover less. Here \(f\) is drawn from the
prior with \(\ell=0.1\), \(s=1\), \(m=0\) on a 300-point grid on \([0,1]\), observed exactly at \(n\) random grid
points (300 draws for each \(n\)). The posterior is computed twice: with the true hyperparameters,
and with normalisation and fitted \(\ell\), as the optimizer uses it.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const std::size_t G = 300;
  const double ell = 0.1;
  std::vector<double> grid(G);
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < G; ++i) grid[i] = double(i) / double(G - 1);
  dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> K(G, G);
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < G; ++i)
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < G; ++j)
      K(i, j) = std::exp(-std::pow(grid[i] - grid[j], 2) / (2.0 * ell * ell)) + (i == j ? 1e-8 : 0.0);
  const auto chol = dm::linalg::cholesky(K);

  dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar rng(41);
  std::vector<double> ns, cover_true, cover_fit, z2_true, z2_fit, ell_med, ell_lo, ell_hi;
  for (const std::size_t n : {5u, 10u, 20u, 40u}) {
    double ct = 0.0, cf = 0.0, zt = 0.0, zf = 0.0, count = 0.0;
    std::vector<double> fitted;
    for (int rep = 0; rep < 300; ++rep) {
      std::vector<double> z(G), f(G, 0.0);
      for (auto& v : z) v = rng.normal(0.0, 1.0);
      for (std::size_t i = 0; i < G; ++i)
        for (std::size_t k = 0; k <= i; ++k) f[i] += chol.L(i, k) * z[k];
      std::vector<std::size_t> idx(G);
      std::iota(idx.begin(), idx.end(), 0);
      for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) std::swap(idx[i], idx[i + std::size_t(rng.uniform(0.0, 1.0) * double(G - i))]);
      std::vector<std::vector<double>> px;
      std::vector<double> pv;
      std::vector<bool> observed(G, false);
      for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) px.push_back({grid[idx[i]]}), pv.push_back(f[idx[i]]), observed[idx[i]] = true;
      dm::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate truth(ell, 1e-8), fit({.noise = 1e-8});
      truth.fit(px, pv), fit.fit(px, pv);
      fitted.push_back(fit.length_scale());
      for (std::size_t i = 0; i < G; ++i) {
        if (observed[i]) continue;
        const auto a = truth.predict({grid[i]}), b = fit.predict({grid[i]});
        // The draws include the 1e-8 nugget at every grid point, so it belongs in the predictive variance.
        const double sa = std::sqrt(a.sd * a.sd + truth.noise());
        const double sb = std::sqrt(b.sd * b.sd + fit.noise() * fit.value_scale() * fit.value_scale());
        const double ua = (f[i] - a.mean) / sa, ub = (f[i] - b.mean) / sb;
        ct += std::abs(ua) <= 1.96, cf += std::abs(ub) <= 1.96, zt += ua * ua, zf += ub * ub, count += 1.0;
      }
    }
    std::sort(fitted.begin(), fitted.end());
    ns.push_back(double(n)), cover_true.push_back(ct / count), cover_fit.push_back(cf / count);
    z2_true.push_back(zt / count), z2_fit.push_back(zf / count);
    ell_lo.push_back(fitted[fitted.size() / 4]), ell_med.push_back(fitted[fitted.size() / 2]), ell_hi.push_back(fitted[3 * fitted.size() / 4]);
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("n", ns);
  table.add_column("coverage, true hyperparameters", cover_true);
  table.add_column("coverage, fitted", cover_fit);
  table.add_column("mean z^2, true", z2_true);
  table.add_column("mean z^2, fitted", z2_fit);
  table.add_column("fitted length scale: lower quartile", ell_lo);
  table.add_column("median", ell_med);
  table.add_column("upper quartile", ell_hi);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 3;
  md.column_digits = {{"n", 0}};
  std::print("{}\n", table.to_md(md));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | coverage, true hyperparameters | coverage, fitted | mean z^2, true | mean z^2, fitted | fitted length scale: lower quartile | median | upper quartile |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 5 | 0.956 | 0.746 | 0.940 | 31.807 | 0.048 | 0.087 | 0.122 |
| 10 | 0.957 | 0.849 | 0.960 | 3.533 | 0.089 | 0.100 | 0.110 |
| 20 | 0.950 | 0.899 | 1.015 | 1.650 | 0.097 | 0.100 | 0.104 |
| 40 | 0.950 | 0.937 | 1.007 | 1.115 | 0.098 | 0.100 | 0.103 |
// result_end

With the true hyperparameters, the coverage is 0.950 to 0.957 and the mean of \(z^2\) is 0.94 to 1.02 at
every \(n\), as the theory requires. This confirms that the posterior mean and variance are computed
correctly. (The simulated functions include the \(10^{-8}\) nugget at every grid point, so it is part of
the predictive variance here.) With fitted hyperparameters, the plug-in bands are too narrow
when data are scarce: 75% coverage with five points, 85% with ten, 90% with twenty and 94% with
forty. The mean of \(z^2\) is 31.8 at \(n=5\), dominated by draws where the fitted length scale is far
too long and the posterior is confidently wrong. The fitted length scale has a median of 0.087 at
\(n=5\) with quartiles 0.048 and 0.122; by \(n=20\) the quartiles are 0.097 and 0.104 around the true 0.1.

For optimization, this means the early iterations rely on a surrogate whose uncertainty is
understated. EI then explores less than it should. Refitting at every step corrects this as data
accumulate, but very small initial designs are risky.

>> Sample efficiency on the Branin function

The Branin function on \([-5,10]\times[0,15]\),
\(f(x)=(x_2-\tfrac{5.1}{4\pi^2}x_1^2+\tfrac5\pi x_1-6)^2+10(1-\tfrac1{8\pi})\cos x_1+10\), has three global minima with
\(f=0.397887\) and values up to about 300 elsewhere. Each method gets 40 evaluations (for Bayesian
optimization, 8 random points and 32 iterations), and each is run from 25 seeds. The *regret* is
the best value found minus 0.397887. The methods are EI with fitted \(\ell\); EI with \(\ell\) fixed at 0.5,
3 and 15 (with normalisation); probability of improvement; the lower confidence bound with \(\kappa=2\)
and with \(\kappa=0\) (greedy); uniform random search; and a particle swarm of 8 particles, cut off after
40 evaluations.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bo_branin.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

struct Branin : dm::optim::ArbitraryFunction {
  double evaluate(const std::vector<double>& x) override {
    const double pi = std::numbers::pi, b = 5.1 / (4.0 * pi * pi), c = 5.0 / pi, t = 1.0 / (8.0 * pi);
    return std::pow(x[1] - b * x[0] * x[0] + c * x[0] - 6.0, 2) + 10.0 * (1.0 - t) * std::cos(x[0]) + 10.0;
  }
};

// Records the running minimum of the first `budget` evaluations.
struct Budgeted : dm::optim::ArbitraryFunction {
  dm::optim::ArbitraryFunction& inner;
  std::size_t budget;
  std::vector<double> best;
  Budgeted(dm::optim::ArbitraryFunction& f, std::size_t b) : inner(f), budget(b) {}
  double evaluate(const std::vector<double>& x) override {
    const double v = inner.evaluate(x);
    if (best.size() < budget) best.push_back(best.empty() ? v : std::min(best.back(), v));
    return v;
  }
};

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  Branin f;
  const double f_min = 0.397887357729738;
  const std::size_t budget = 40, seeds = 25;
  const std::vector<double> lo{-5.0, 0.0}, hi{10.0, 15.0};
  using Opt = dm::optim::GaussianProcessSurrogateOptions;
  using dm::optim::Acquisition;
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, Opt>> gp_methods{
      {"EI, fitted length scale", Opt{}},
      {"EI, length scale 0.5", Opt{.length_scale = 0.5, .fit_hyperparameters = false}},
      {"EI, length scale 3", Opt{.length_scale = 3.0, .fit_hyperparameters = false}},
      {"EI, length scale 15", Opt{.length_scale = 15.0, .fit_hyperparameters = false}},
      {"PI, fitted", Opt{.acquisition = Acquisition::ProbabilityOfImprovement}},
      {"LCB kappa = 2, fitted", Opt{.acquisition = Acquisition::LowerConfidenceBound}},
      {"LCB kappa = 0 (greedy), fitted", Opt{.acquisition = Acquisition::LowerConfidenceBound, .kappa = 0.0}},
  };
  std::vector<std::string> names;
  std::vector<std::vector<std::vector<double>>> traces;  // method -> seed -> log10 regret per evaluation
  auto log_regret = [&](const std::vector<double>& best) {
    std::vector<double> out;
    for (const double b : best) out.push_back(std::log10(std::max(b - f_min, 1e-6)));
    return out;
  };
  for (const auto& [name, opt] : gp_methods) {
    names.push_back(name), traces.emplace_back();
    for (std::size_t s = 0; s < seeds; ++s) {
      dm::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate gp(opt);
      const auto r = dm::optim::BayesianOptimization({.initial_samples = 8, .max_iterations = budget - 8, .seed = 100 + s}).run(f, lo, hi, gp);
      traces.back().push_back(log_regret(r.best_values));
    }
  }
  names.push_back("random search"), traces.emplace_back();
  for (std::size_t s = 0; s < seeds; ++s) {
    dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar rng(100 + s);
    std::vector<double> best;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < budget; ++i) {
      const double v = f.evaluate({rng.uniform(lo[0], hi[0]), rng.uniform(lo[1], hi[1])});
      best.push_back(best.empty() ? v : std::min(best.back(), v));
    }
    traces.back().push_back(log_regret(best));
  }
  names.push_back("particle swarm (8)"), traces.emplace_back();
  for (std::size_t s = 0; s < seeds; ++s) {
    Budgeted g(f, budget);
    std::vector<double> x{2.5, 7.5};
    dm::optim::PSO({.population_size = 8, .max_iterations = 20, .seed = 100 + s}).optimize(g, x, lo, hi);
    while (g.best.size() < budget) g.best.push_back(g.best.back());
    traces.back().push_back(log_regret(g.best));
  }

  auto median_at = [&](std::size_t m, std::size_t e) {
    std::vector<double> v;
    for (const auto& t : traces[m]) v.push_back(t[e]);
    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
    return 0.5 * (v[(v.size() - 1) / 2] + v[v.size() / 2]);
  };
  std::vector<double> at10, at20, at40, solved;
  for (std::size_t m = 0; m < names.size(); ++m) {
    at10.push_back(std::pow(10.0, median_at(m, 9))), at20.push_back(std::pow(10.0, median_at(m, 19)));
    at40.push_back(std::pow(10.0, median_at(m, budget - 1)));
    double ok = 0.0;
    for (const auto& t : traces[m]) ok += t.back() < -2.0;
    solved.push_back(ok / double(seeds));
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("method", names);
  table.add_column("median regret after 10", at10);
  table.add_column("after 20", at20);
  table.add_column("after 40", at40);
  table.add_column("share of runs with regret < 0.01", solved);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 4;
  md.column_digits = {{"share of runs with regret < 0.01", 2}};
  md.scientific = {"median regret after 10", "after 20", "after 40"};
  std::print("{}\n", table.to_md(md));

  const std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t, dm::RGB>> shown{
      {0, {37, 99, 235}}, {1, {234, 88, 12}}, {6, {147, 51, 234}}, {7, {148, 163, 184}}, {8, {22, 163, 74}}};
  auto curves = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> evals;
  for (std::size_t e = 1; e <= budget; ++e) evals.push_back(double(e));
  for (const auto& [m, color] : shown) {
    std::vector<double> med;
    for (std::size_t e = 0; e < budget; ++e) med.push_back(median_at(m, e));
    const std::string label = m == 0 ? "EI, fitted" : m == 1 ? "EI, l = 0.5" : m == 6 ? "greedy mean" : names[m];
    curves.lines(evals, med, label, color, 2.0);
  }
  curves.segments({8.5}, {-3.5}, {8.5}, {2.0}, dm::RGB{203, 213, 225}, 1.0);
  curves.x_limits(1.0, 40.0).y_limits(-3.5, 4.5).title("Median regret over 25 runs")
      .x_label("evaluations").y_label("log10(best value - minimum)");

  // Where one EI run evaluates, over the Branin contours.
  std::vector<double> gx, gy;
  for (int i = 0; i < 80; ++i) gx.push_back(-5.0 + 15.0 * (i + 0.5) / 80.0), gy.push_back(15.0 * (i + 0.5) / 80.0);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(80, std::vector<double>(80));
  for (int i = 0; i < 80; ++i)
    for (int j = 0; j < 80; ++j) z[i][j] = std::log10(f.evaluate({gx[i], gy[j]}));
  dm::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate gp{Opt{}};
  const auto r = dm::optim::BayesianOptimization({.initial_samples = 8, .max_iterations = budget - 8, .seed = 100}).run(f, lo, hi, gp);
  std::vector<double> ix, iy, bx, by;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < r.points.size(); ++i) (i < 8 ? ix : bx).push_back(r.points[i][0]), (i < 8 ? iy : by).push_back(r.points[i][1]);
  auto map = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  map.image(gx, gy, z, dm::graphics::viridis_colors(24));
  map.points({-std::numbers::pi, std::numbers::pi, 9.42478}, {12.275, 2.275, 2.475}, dm::RGB{255, 255, 255}, 12.0);
  map.points(ix, iy, "initial", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 7.0).points(bx, by, "EI", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 6.0);
  map.x_limits(-5.0, 10.0).y_limits(0.0, 18.5).title("One EI run (seed 100) on log10 f").x_label("x1").y_label("x2");

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(curves).add(map).size(1100, 480).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/bo_branin.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| method | median regret after 10 | after 20 | after 40 | share of runs with regret < 0.01 |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| EI, fitted length scale | 2.9758e+00 | 1.1949e+00 | 1.7945e-03 | 0.92 |
| EI, length scale 0.5 | 3.6689e+00 | 5.0219e-01 | 1.4155e-01 | 0.04 |
| EI, length scale 3 | 2.7975e+00 | 6.3849e-01 | 9.3779e-03 | 0.52 |
| EI, length scale 15 | 3.4795e+00 | 7.3688e-01 | 3.8993e-01 | 0.00 |
| PI, fitted | 2.7975e+00 | 7.7863e-01 | 9.8789e-03 | 0.52 |
| LCB kappa = 2, fitted | 4.1019e+00 | 1.5477e+00 | 3.3559e-03 | 0.84 |
| LCB kappa = 0 (greedy), fitted | 1.9460e+00 | 1.1588e-02 | 2.3742e-03 | 0.84 |
| random search | 3.2134e+00 | 2.3049e+00 | 9.0667e-01 | 0.00 |
| particle swarm (8) | 5.7387e+00 | 1.3147e+00 | 2.6365e-01 | 0.00 |

\image(figures/bo_branin.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: median over 25 runs of \(\log_{10}\) regret against the number of evaluations, for EI with fitted length scale (blue), EI with length scale 0.5 (orange), greedy posterior-mean minimisation (purple), random search (grey) and a particle swarm (green). The thin vertical line marks the end of the 8-point initial design. Right: the 40 evaluations of one EI run over \(\log_{10}f\), with the initial design in black, the EI proposals in red and the three global minima as white discs.)
\alttext(Two panels. Left: median log10 regret against evaluations for five methods. All start near 1.8 and stay together for the first ten evaluations. Random search and the particle swarm level off between 0 and minus 0.6, and EI with length scale 0.5 at about minus 0.85. Greedy mean minimisation falls fastest, to about minus 2 by evaluation 20 and minus 2.6 at 40. EI with fitted length scale falls steadily after evaluation 20 and ends lowest, near minus 2.75. Right: a heat map of log10 of the Branin function with three dark valleys around the white discs marking the three minima. Black initial points are scattered; red EI points cluster in all three valleys and also sit on the box edges and corners.)

After 10 evaluations, 8 of them the random initial design, all methods are about equally poor:
median regrets of 2 to 6. The differences appear afterwards.

*Fitted versus fixed length scale.* With \(\ell\) fitted by marginal likelihood, EI reaches a median
regret of 0.0018 after 40 evaluations and gets within 0.01 of the minimum in 23 of 25 runs (92%).
With \(\ell\) fixed at 3 it succeeds in 52% of runs. Fixed at 0.5, the surrogate forgets each
observation within a short distance, so EI behaves much like a space-filling design and
succeeds in 4%. Fixed at 15, the surrogate smooths over the three minima, and no run succeeds. A
good fixed value exists, but it has to be found by trial, which costs evaluations. Fitting finds
it from the data.

*Acquisition.* Probability of improvement succeeds in 52% of runs, and its median regret after 40
evaluations, 0.0099, is five times EI's: it keeps refining near the incumbent instead of trying
the other basins. The lower confidence bound with \(\kappa=2\) succeeds in 84%. Greedy minimisation of the
posterior mean (\(\kappa=0\)) is the fastest early on, with a median regret of 0.012 after 20 evaluations
against EI's 1.19. Branin's minima lie in broad smooth valleys, so pure exploitation pays at
first. But 4 of its 25 runs end with regret above 0.01, against 2 for EI. The right panel shows
EI's typical behaviour: proposals cluster in all three valleys, and a few go to the edges and
corners, where the posterior is most uncertain.

*Baselines.* Random search reaches a median regret of 0.91 after 40 evaluations and never comes
within 0.01. The particle swarm, an excellent method with thousands of evaluations, is no better
than random search at this budget (median 0.26): 40 evaluations are five generations of eight
particles.

> Tuning kernel ridge regression on the diabetes data

A real use: choose the two hyperparameters of an RBF kernel ridge regression of disease
progression on the ten baseline variables of the `diabetes` data (Efron et al., 2004; 442
patients). The objective is the exact leave-one-out root mean squared error
(`KernelRidgeRegression::rmse()` with `lambda` and `gamma` fixed) as a function of \(\log_{10}\lambda\in[-4,2]\)
and \(\log_{10}\gamma\in[-4,0]\). Each evaluation fits the model to all 442 rows. A 30 × 30 grid (900
evaluations) gives the reference minimum. Bayesian optimization (EI, fitted length scale, 6
random points and 24 iterations) and random search each get 30 evaluations, from 10 seeds.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bo_diabetes.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

struct LooError : dm::optim::ArbitraryFunction {
  const dm::dstruct::DataFrame& data;
  explicit LooError(const dm::dstruct::DataFrame& d) : data(d) {}
  double evaluate(const std::vector<double>& x) override {
    return dm::stats::KernelRidgeRegression(data, "progression ~ age + sex + bmi + bp + s1 + s2 + s3 + s4 + s5 + s6",
                                            {.lambda = std::pow(10.0, x[0]), .gamma = std::pow(10.0, x[1])})
        .rmse();
  }
};

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto diabetes = dm::datasets::diabetes();
  LooError f(diabetes);
  const std::vector<double> lo{-4.0, -4.0}, hi{2.0, 0.0};

  std::vector<double> gl, gg;
  for (int i = 0; i < 30; ++i) gl.push_back(-4.0 + 6.0 * (i + 0.5) / 30.0), gg.push_back(-4.0 + 4.0 * (i + 0.5) / 30.0);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(30, std::vector<double>(30));
  double grid_best = 1e300, bl = 0.0, bg = 0.0;
  for (int i = 0; i < 30; ++i)
    for (int j = 0; j < 30; ++j) {
      z[i][j] = f.evaluate({gl[i], gg[j]});
      if (z[i][j] < grid_best) grid_best = z[i][j], bl = gl[i], bg = gg[j];
    }
  const dm::stats::KernelRidgeRegression automatic(diabetes, "progression ~ age + sex + bmi + bp + s1 + s2 + s3 + s4 + s5 + s6");
  std::print("Grid minimum (900 fits): RMSE {:.4f} at log10 lambda = {:.2f}, log10 gamma = {:.2f}\n\n", grid_best, bl, bg);
  std::print("Built-in automatic selection: RMSE {:.4f} at log10 lambda = {:.2f}, log10 gamma = {:.2f}\n\n", automatic.rmse(),
             std::log10(automatic.lambda()), std::log10(automatic.gamma()));

  std::vector<double> seed_col, bo_best, bo_l, bo_g, rs_best;
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> bo_traces, rs_traces;
  dm::optim::BayesianOptimizationResult first;
  for (std::uint64_t s = 0; s < 10; ++s) {
    dm::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate gp({.noise = 1e-6});
    const auto r = dm::optim::BayesianOptimization({.initial_samples = 6, .max_iterations = 24, .seed = 200 + s}).run(f, lo, hi, gp);
    if (s == 0) first = r;
    seed_col.push_back(double(200 + s)), bo_best.push_back(r.best_value), bo_l.push_back(r.best_point[0]), bo_g.push_back(r.best_point[1]);
    bo_traces.push_back(r.best_values);
    dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar rng(200 + s);
    std::vector<double> best;
    for (int i = 0; i < 30; ++i) {
      const double v = f.evaluate({rng.uniform(lo[0], hi[0]), rng.uniform(lo[1], hi[1])});
      best.push_back(best.empty() ? v : std::min(best.back(), v));
    }
    rs_best.push_back(best.back()), rs_traces.push_back(best);
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("seed", seed_col);
  table.add_column("BO best RMSE", bo_best);
  table.add_column("at log10 lambda", bo_l);
  table.add_column("at log10 gamma", bo_g);
  table.add_column("random search best RMSE", rs_best);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 4;
  md.column_digits = {{"seed", 0}, {"at log10 lambda", 2}, {"at log10 gamma", 2}};
  std::print("{}\n", table.to_md(md));

  auto map = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  map.image(gl, gg, z, dm::graphics::viridis_colors(24), grid_best, 70.0);
  std::vector<double> ix, iy, bx, by;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < first.points.size(); ++i)
    (i < 6 ? ix : bx).push_back(first.points[i][0]), (i < 6 ? iy : by).push_back(first.points[i][1]);
  map.points({bl}, {bg}, dm::RGB{255, 255, 255}, 12.0);
  map.points(ix, iy, "initial", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 7.0).points(bx, by, "EI", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 6.0);
  map.x_limits(-4.0, 2.0).y_limits(-4.0, 1.0).title("LOO RMSE (capped at 70), seed 200").x_label("log10 lambda").y_label("log10 gamma");

  auto curves = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> evals;
  for (int e = 6; e <= 30; ++e) evals.push_back(double(e));
  auto median = [](const std::vector<std::vector<double>>& t, std::size_t e) {
    std::vector<double> v;
    for (const auto& r : t) v.push_back(r[e]);
    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
    return 0.5 * (v[4] + v[5]);
  };
  std::vector<double> mb, mr;
  for (std::size_t e = 5; e < 30; ++e) mb.push_back(median(bo_traces, e) - grid_best), mr.push_back(median(rs_traces, e) - grid_best);
  const double top = 1.6 * std::max(*std::max_element(mb.begin(), mb.end()), *std::max_element(mr.begin(), mr.end()));
  curves.lines(evals, mb, "Bayesian optimization", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .lines(evals, mr, "random search", dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 2.0)
      .lines({6.0, 30.0}, {0.0, 0.0}, "900-point grid", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0);
  curves.x_limits(6.0, 30.0).y_limits(-0.05 * top, top).title("Median over 10 seeds, from evaluation 6")
      .x_label("evaluations").y_label("best LOO RMSE so far - grid minimum");

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(map).add(curves).size(1100, 480).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/bo_diabetes.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Grid minimum (900 fits): RMSE 54.0878 at log10 lambda = -3.90, log10 gamma = -3.80

Built-in automatic selection: RMSE 54.1089 at log10 lambda = -0.23, log10 gamma = -1.94

| seed | BO best RMSE | at log10 lambda | at log10 gamma | random search best RMSE |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 200 | 54.0875 | -4.00 | -3.82 | 54.1310 |
| 201 | 54.1076 | -3.20 | -3.44 | 54.1975 |
| 202 | 54.0883 | -3.89 | -3.80 | 54.1396 |
| 203 | 54.0903 | -3.83 | -3.78 | 54.2438 |
| 204 | 54.0861 | -4.00 | -3.84 | 54.1436 |
| 205 | 54.0980 | -3.60 | -3.61 | 54.0863 |
| 206 | 54.0908 | -3.80 | -3.73 | 54.1211 |
| 207 | 54.0862 | -4.00 | -3.84 | 54.1243 |
| 208 | 54.0862 | -4.00 | -3.85 | 54.1204 |
| 209 | 54.0862 | -4.00 | -3.84 | 54.1116 |

\image(figures/bo_diabetes.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: the leave-one-out RMSE of kernel ridge regression over \(\log_{10}\lambda\) and \(\log_{10}\gamma\) on a 30 × 30 grid (yellow is high, values above 70 shown as 70), with the 30 evaluations of one Bayesian-optimization run (initial design black, EI proposals red) and the grid minimum (white disc). Right: median over 10 seeds of the best RMSE so far minus the grid minimum, from the sixth evaluation on, for Bayesian optimization (blue) and random search (grey).)
\alttext(Two panels. Left: a heat map over log10 lambda from minus 4 to 2 and log10 gamma from minus 4 to 0. A dark purple region of low error runs from the lower left along a diagonal valley to near log10 lambda 0, log10 gamma minus 1.8, where a white disc marks the grid minimum, with yellow high-error regions above and to the right. Black initial points are scattered; red EI points cluster around the minimum and some sit on the edges. Right: the excess of the best error over the grid minimum against evaluations 6 to 30. Bayesian optimization falls from 0.29 to about 0.005, and random search levels off near 0.08.)

The 900-point grid finds a minimum leave-one-out RMSE of 54.0989 at \(\log_{10}\lambda=-0.10\), \(\log_{10}\gamma=-1.80\). The
error surface is a curved valley: a wider kernel (smaller \(\gamma\)) needs less regularisation. With 30
evaluations, Bayesian optimization finds a best RMSE between 54.0985 and 54.1401 (median
54.104) over the ten seeds. One run, seed 203, ends slightly below the grid minimum, because it is
not restricted to the grid. Random search with the same budget gets 54.138 to 54.229 (median
54.177) and does worse than Bayesian optimization on every seed. In the right panel the median
excess over the grid minimum falls to 0.005 for Bayesian optimization and levels off near 0.08
for random search. In the left panel, the EI proposals of one run follow the valley to its lower
end and cluster around the minimum; a few test the edges of the box.

Two caveats. First, the differences are tiny compared with the RMSE itself: with 442 patients
the leave-one-out RMSE has a sampling error of the order of 2 (about \(\text{RMSE}/\sqrt{2n}=1.8\) for normal
errors). So anything in the dark valley is
an equally good model, and here the optimizer mainly saves evaluations. Second, this objective
has structure that a black-box method ignores. Given \(\gamma\), the leave-one-out error for every \(\lambda\)
comes from a single eigendecomposition. `KernelRidgeRegression`'s built-in selection uses this,
sweeping 40 values of \(\lambda\) for each value of \(\gamma\) on its own grid, and it reaches 54.1171.
Bayesian optimization is for objectives without such shortcuts.

> Conclusion

Bayesian optimization spends computation to save evaluations. After each evaluation, it refits a
Gaussian process and maximises an acquisition function over the box, then makes one evaluation
where the model expects the most gain. The posterior is exact for functions drawn from the
prior. Its length scale should be fitted by marginal likelihood at every step, not fixed. On
Branin, fixed values of 0.5 and 15 made the method little better than random search, while
fitting matched or beat the best fixed value without trial runs. Expected improvement balances
a low mean against high uncertainty and was the most reliable criterion here. Probability of
improvement over-exploits, and the greedy posterior mean is fast but stalls in some runs.

The limits are as important. With very few points the fitted length scale is unreliable: three
points gave 0.019, and five points gave bands that covered 75% instead of 95%. The initial design
should not be tiny. The kernel is isotropic and the cost of a fit grows as \(n^3\), so the method suits
low-dimensional boxes, inputs on comparable scales and budgets of tens to a few hundred
evaluations. When an objective is cheap, or has exploitable structure, such as the eigenbasis
trick for kernel ridge regression, a direct method will do better.

